+## 2. Full-Spectrum Evaluations
+
+### R13 · x402 — HTTP-native payments for AI agents — `adapt_cleanroom`
+
+**Evidence trail:** Coinbase Development Platform + Cloudflare launched x402 publicly in
+**September 2025** (fintechwrapup 2025-11-08; dwellir 2026-02-13); governance moved to the
+neutral **x402 Foundation, fully operational July 2026** (clinkbill 2026-08-24). The
+protocol reuses HTTP `402 Payment Required` + `X-PAYMENT` headers so any HTTP client —
+including autonomous agents — can pay per call; Solana and Base integrations exist
+(solana.com/x402 docs).
+
+- **Pros:** the protocol surface is two headers and a JSON body — implementable natively,
+  zero-dependency, exactly the Surgical Extraction doctrine's shape; per-call metering is
+  the missing monetization primitive for a plugin registry; neutral foundation governance
+  (not vendor-owned) after July 2026 lowers single-vendor risk.
+- **Cons:** stablecoin-denominated by default (`asset` field) — currency and chain are
+  data the operator must choose; ecosystem tooling is young; governance neutrality is
+  months old, not years.
+- **Risks:** any auto-pay wiring turns every agent loop into a spending loop; custody of
+  keys anywhere in satellite code is an unacceptable breach surface; spec churn during
+  early-foundation phase.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** hard-coding a chain/token in satellite logic; agents that
+  pay without per-call budget ledgers; "monetize by default" toggles.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** SupremeAI MUST NOT hold user wallet custody, MUST NOT auto-pay
+  without an explicit budget ledger (P-11), and MUST NOT couple satellite code to any
+  specific chain. Payment data stays data.
+- **Adoption path → Issue [#1647](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/issues/1647):**
+  `billing` block in the `mcp_plugins` registry schema (synergy with WO #1523, P-09),
+  gateway passes 402 challenges through verbatim, no auto-pay in v1.
+
+### R14 · OpenTelemetry GenAI semantic conventions — `accept_verbatim` (taxonomy)
+
+**Evidence trail:** OTel GenAI SIG (working since 2024-04) standardizes the `gen_ai.*`
+namespace for LLM client spans, agent spans (`invoke_agent`, `create_agent`, `plan`),
+tool execution (`execute_tool`), retrieval, and memory ops; token-usage metrics
+(`gen_ai.client.token.usage` etc.). As of **semconv v1.42.0 (mid-2026)** the conventions
+moved to a dedicated repo `open-telemetry/semantic-conventions-genai`; every attribute is
+still **Development** stability (opentelemetry.io; lyzr.ai explainer; Integration
+Plumbers SIG deep-dive 2026-07-20; zylos research 2026-02-28).
+
+- **Pros:** vendor-neutral by construction — the exact property SupremeAI's
+  provider-agnostic routing needs; token/cost attributes make per-task cost accounting
+  (WO #1363) joinable across gateway, loop, and satellites; span-tree shape matches the
+  federation's own hierarchy (agent → plan → tool).
+- **Cons:** Development stability means renames WILL happen (the registry already
+  deprecated `gen_ai.system` for `gen_ai.provider.name`); content capture via span events
+  needs privacy discipline (prompts are PII magnets).
+- **Risks:** adopting the taxonomy into dashboards directly (instead of an adapter layer)
+  couples SupremeAI to upstream churn; partial adoption recreates the fragmentation the
+  convention exists to end.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** logging prompt/completion content into span attributes;
+  vendor dashboard lock-in; pretending Development-stability names are stable.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no prompt/completion content in attributes; no new runtime
+  dependency to adopt a naming convention; no "stable" claims in docs while upstream
+  marks Development.
+- **Adoption path → Issue [#1648](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/issues/1648):**
+  clean-room adapter table (internal event → `gen_ai.*`), pinned semconv version in one
+  module, token-usage fields aligned so P-01/P-11 budget ledgers become joinable.
+
+### R15 · A2A protocol → Agentic AI Foundation — `quarantine_eval`
+
+**Evidence trail:** Google launched A2A at Cloud Next **April 2025** (50+ partners);
+donated to the Linux Foundation **June 2025** (palma.ai handover analysis); moved into
+the **Agentic AI Foundation August 2026** (Axios 2026-08-17). Platform-layer adoption is
+broad in 2026 (360digitaltransformation 2026-09-12). A2A standardizes agent↔agent task
+delegation and discovery (Agent Cards) — complementary to MCP's agent↔tool scope.
+
+- **Pros:** cross-federation task handoff is a real future lane for SupremeAI
+  (federated dispatch already exists internally); Agent Cards are a clean capability-
+  disclosure pattern; neutral governance continues.
+- **Cons:** governance moved twice in 16 months (Google → LF → AAF); spec surface still
+  moving; agent↔agent trust is an unsolved governance problem the federation has not
+  drilled for (P-05 pending).
+- **Risks:** premature adoption couples the federation to a moving spec; public Agent
+  Cards can leak internal topology (the exact class of leak the protected-path rules
+  guard against); A2A adoption theater displacing MCP work that already has landed value.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** "A2A replaces MCP" maximalism; exposing internal endpoints
+  in discovery surfaces; adopting on governance news instead of evidence.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no satellite implements A2A before an RFC decision; no Agent Card
+  publishing internal endpoints or credentials; MCP stays the SSOT for tool access.
+- **Adoption path → Issue [#1649](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/issues/1649):**
+  RFC-only slice — role mapping (Tower/satellites ↔ A2A concepts), explicit coexistence
+  stance, and a MUST-NOT-EXPOSE inventory. Stays `quarantine_eval` until the RFC lands
+  and P-05 covers agent-to-agent trust drills.
+
+### R16 · Firecracker microVM as the agent-sandbox baseline — `adapt_cleanroom`
+
+**Evidence trail:** 2026 comparison guides converge on the isolation gradient
+**containers < gVisor < Firecracker microVMs** for untrusted agent code execution
+(Northflank 2026-02-02; e2b 2026-10-15 — "every agent in its own Firecracker microVM";
+addozhang design-principles breakdown). Firecracker-per-agent is the production baseline
+for untrusted workloads across the sandbox-provider ecosystem.
+
+- **Pros:** the threat model matches SupremeAI's trajectory — marketplace plugins
+  (#1523) and model-authored code through the execution bridge are untrusted by
+  definition; microVM tier is declarative configuration, not a new runtime; the gradient
+  gives an honest cost/security trade-off table instead of a one-size answer.
+- **Cons:** microVM startup/teardown cost is real for short-lived calls; operational
+  maturity required (image hygiene, network policy); the tier must be fail-closed or it
+  becomes decorative.
+- **Risks:** DIY sandboxing ("just namespace it") — the guides exist because DIY loses;
+  shared long-lived sandboxes recreate cross-tenant state bleed (the always-on
+  anti-pattern R2 banned); untrusted tiers with network egress are exfiltration channels.
+- **Anti-patterns flagged:** opting untrusted code down a tier for convenience; per-call
+  container reuse across tenants; egress-by-default from isolation tiers.
+- **এটা করা যাবে না:** no untrusted execution path may opt itself down a tier; no egress
+  without allowlist + logged decision; no tenant-shared persistent sandboxes, ever.
+- **Adoption path → Issue [#1650](https://github.com/Supremeai-bd/supremeai-open/issues/1650):**
+  three-tier isolation profile in the exec-bridge contract (config, not runtime),
+  fail-closed defaults, escape-drill rehearsal added to P-05's harness scope.
